nts. So 5 scheduled, 1 unscheduled.\n\nWhat if we skip C and schedule D, E, F instead? A (540-600), B (600-660), D (660-720), E (720-780), F (780-840) = 5 events scheduled, C unscheduled. Same count.\n\nWhat about scheduling D, E, F first? D (540-600), E (600-660), F (660-720), A (720-780), B (780-840) = 5, C unscheduled. Same.\n\nThe count is always 5 because we have 6 events and can fit 5 in the time window. The ordering doesn't matter for the count.\n\nOK, so the greedy topological sort approach should work well. Let me think about when the ordering actually matters...\n\nActually, the more I think about it, the more I think the key optimization is:\n1. Good ordering (process constrained events first)\n2. For events with prerequisites, try to place them as early as possible after their prerequisites to leave room for others\n3. When an event can't be placed, that's legitimate\n\nFor a simple but effective optimization approach:\n1. Topologically sort\n2. Within each level, sort by constraint tightness\n3. Place each event as early as possible (to leave room for later events)\n4. This greedy approach should be optimal or near-optimal for most cases\n\nNow, one more thing to consider: the preferred room. It's a \"hint, not hard constraint.\" So we should try to honor it, but if the preferred room doesn't work, we should try other rooms.\n\nLet me implement this now.\n\nActually, I also want to handle the case where gaps between prerequisite events need to be respected even when they're in different rooms. The problem says: \"The gap between prerequisite events also applies (A's end time + gap <= B's start time).\" So this is a time constraint, not just a room constraint.\n\nLet me write the implementation:\n\n```typescript\nexport interface Event {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  duration: number;\n  attendees?: number;\n  prerequisites?: string[];\n  preferredRoom?: string;\n}\n\nexport interface Room {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  capacity: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ScheduleOptions {\n  startTime: number;\n  endTime: number;\n  gap?: number;\n}\n\nexport interface Assignment {\n  eventId: string;\n  roomId: string;\n  startTime: number;\n  endTime: number;\n}\n\nexport interface ScheduleResult {\n  assignments: Assignment[];\n  unscheduled: string[];\n}\n\nfunction detectCycle(events: Event[]): boolean {\n  const eventMap = new Map(events.map(e => [e.id, e]));\n  const visited = new Set<string>();\n  const inStack = new Set<string>();\n  \n  function dfs(eventId: string): boolean {\n    if (inStack.has(eventId)) return true;\n    if (visited.has(eventId)) return false;\n    \n    visited.add(eventId);\n    inStack.add(eventId);\n    \n    const event = eventMap.get(eventId);\n    if (event?.prerequisites) {\n      for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n        if (prereqId === eventId) return true; // self-reference\n        if (dfs(prereqId)) return true;\n      }\n    }\n    \n    inStack.delete(eventId);\n    return false;\n  }\n  \n  for (const event of events) {\n    if (dfs(event.id)) return true;\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n\n// Topological sort using Kahn's algorithm\nfunction topologicalSort(events: Event[]): Event[] {\n  const eventMap = new Map(events.map(e => [e.id, e]));\n  const inDegree = new Map<string, number>();\n  const adjList = new Map<string, string[]>();\n  \n  // Initialize\n  for (const event of events) {\n    inDegree.set(event.id, 0);\n    adjList.set(event.id, []);\n  }\n  \n  // Build graph\n  for (const event of events) {\n    if (event.prerequisites) {\n      for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n        // prereqId -> event.id (prereq must finish before event starts)\n        adjList.get(prereqId)?.push(event.id);\n        inDegree.set(event.id, (inDegree.get(event.id) || 0) + 1);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Kahn's algorithm\n  const queue: string[] = [];\n  for (const [id, degree] of inDegree) {\n    if (degree === 0) {\n      queue.push(id);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const sorted: Event[] = [];\n  while (queue.length > 0) {\n    // Sort queue by constraint tightness for better scheduling\n    queue.sort((a, b) => {\n      const ea = eventMap.get(a)!;\n      const eb = eventMap.get(b)!;\n      // More constrained events first\n      const capA = ea.attendees || 1;\n      const capB = eb.attendees || 1;\n      if (capA !== capB) return capB - capA; // larger capacity needs first\n      return ea.duration - eb.duration; // shorter first\n    });\n    \n    const id = queue.shift()!;\n    sorted.push(eventMap.get(id)!);\n    \n    for (const neighbor of adjList.get(id) || []) {\n      const newDegree = (inDegree.get(neighbor) || 1) - 1;\n      inDegree.set(neighbor, newDegree);\n      if (newDegree === 0) {\n        queue.push(neighbor);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return sorted;\n}\n\n// Room scheduling state\nclass RoomSchedule {\n  private intervals: Array<{ start: number; end: number; eventId: string }> = [];\n  \n  constructor(public room: Room) {}\n  \n  getEarliestValidStartTime(\n    duration: number,\n    earliestStart: number,\n    options: ScheduleOptions,\n    gap: number\n  ): number | null {\n    // Sort intervals by start time\n    this.intervals.sort((a, b) => a.start - b.start);\n    \n    let candidateStart = Math.max(earliestStart, options.startTime);\n    const endTime = candidateStart + duration;\n    \n    if (endTime > options.endTime) return null;\n    \n    // Check if candidateStart conflicts with any existing interval\n    for (const interval of this.intervals) {\n      // Candidate ends before next interval starts (with gap)\n      if (candidateStart + duration + gap <= interval.start) {\n        return candidateStart;\n      }\n      // If there's overlap, try after this interval + gap\n      if (candidateStart < interval.end + gap) {\n        candidateStart = interval.end + gap;\n        if (candidateStart + duration > options.endTime) {\n          return null;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    return candidateStart;\n  }\n  \n  placeEvent(eventId: string, startTime: number, endTime: number): void {\n    this.intervals.push({ start: startTime, end: endTime, eventId });\n  }\n}\n\nfunction schedule(events: Event[], rooms: Room[], options: ScheduleOptions): ScheduleResult {\n  // Set defaults\n  const gap = options.gap ?? 15;\n  \n  // Step 1: Cycle detection\n  if (detectCycle(events)) {\n    throw new Error(\"Prerequisite graph contains a cycle\");\n  }\n  \n  // Step 2: Topological sort\n  const sortedEvents = topologicalSort(events);\n  \n  // Step 3: Create initial event map and room states\n  const eventMap = new Map(events.map(e => [e.id, e]));\n  const roomSchedules = new Map(rooms.map(r => [r.id, new RoomSchedule(r)]));\n  const assignments: Assignment[] = [];\n  const unscheduled: string[] = [];\n  const scheduledEvents = new Set<string>();\n  \n  // Track finish times of events (for prerequisite checking)\n  const finishTimes = new Map<string, number>();\n  \n  // Step 4: Schedule each event\n  for (const event of sortedEvents) {\n    const attendees = event.attendees ?? 1;\n    \n    // Find eligible rooms\n    let eligibleRooms = rooms.filter(r => r.capacity >= attendees);\n    \n    if (eligibleRooms.length === 0) {\n      unscheduled.push(event.id);\n      continue;\n    }\n    \n    // Determine earliest start time based on prerequisites\n    let earliestStart = options.startTime;\n    if (event.prerequisites && event.prerequisites.length > 0) {\n      let maxFinish = options.startTime;\n      for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n        const finishTime = finishTimes.get(prereqId);\n        if (finishTime !== undefined) {\n          maxFinish = Math.max(maxFinish, finishTime);\n        }\n        // If prerequisite isn't scheduled, we might have an issue\n        // But it should be scheduled since topological sort ensures order\n      }\n      earliestStart = maxFinish + gap;\n    }\n    \n    // Try each eligible room to find the best placement\n    let bestAssignment: { roomId: string; startTime: number; endTime: number } | null = null;\n    \n    // Sort eligible rooms: preferred first, then by capacity fit\n    eligibleRooms.sort((a, b) => {\n      if (event.preferredRoom) {\n        if (a.id === event.preferredRoom) return -1;\n        if (b.id === event.preferredRoom) return 1;\n      }\n      // Prefer room with tightest fit (minimize wasted capacity)\n      const wasteA = a.capacity - attendees;\n      const wasteB = b.capacity - attendees;\n      return wasteA - wasteB;\n    });\n    \n    for (const room of eligibleRooms) {\n      const schedule = roomSchedules.get(room.id)!;\n      const validStart = schedule.getEarliestValidStartTime(\n        event.duration,\n        earliestStart,\n        options,\n        gap\n      );\n      \n      if (validStart !== null) {\n        const endTime = validStart + event.duration;\n        // Prefer earlier start times\n        if (!bestAssignment || validStart < bestAssignment.startTime) {\n          bestAssignment = { roomId: room.id, startTime: validStart, endTime };\n        }\n        // If we found a placement in the preferred room, use it immediately\n        if (room.id === event.preferredRoom) {\n          break;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    if (bestAssignment) {\n      const schedule = roomSchedules.get(bestAssignment.roomId)!;\n      schedule.placeEvent(event.id, bestAssignment.startTime, bestAssignment.endTime);\n      finishTimes.set(event.id, bestAssignment.endTime);\n      assignments.push({\n        eventId: event.id,\n        roomId: bestAssignment.roomId,\n        startTime: bestAssignment.startTime,\n        endTime: bestAssignment.endTime,\n      });\n      scheduledEvents.add(event.id);\n    } else {\n      unscheduled.push(event.id);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return { assignments, unscheduled };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the cycle detection. The current implementation has a subtle issue: it only checks for reachable cycles from each starting node, but it doesn't handle the case where a node's prerequisite doesn't exist in the events list. Let me think about this...\n\nAlso, the prerequisites define \"A must finish before B starts\", meaning the graph edges go from prerequisite to dependent event. For cycle detection, we need to follow the graph from a node to its prerequisites.\n\nLet me also reconsider the topological sort. In Kahn's algorithm, edges represent \"prerequisite -> dependent\", so:\n- If A is a prerequisite of B, then A must come before B\n- In the graph: A -> B (edge from A to B)\n- inDegree[B] counts how many prerequisites B has\n\nWait, actually in my code, I'm not handling the edge direction correctly. Let me fix this:\n\nThe dependency graph: if B has prerequisite [A], then A must finish before B starts.\nSo in the topo sort, A comes before B.\n\nFor Kahn's algorithm:\n- Edge: A -> B (A must be before B)\n- inDegree: number of incoming edges (prerequisites)\n- Initially: nodes with no prerequisites have inDegree 0\n- For each edge A -> B, inDegree[B]++\n\nIn my code, I iterate over event.prerequisites and add edges from prereqId to event.id. That's correct: edge prereqId -> event.id, meaning prereqId comes before event.id. And inDegree[event.id] should increment.\n\nLet me trace through my code:\n```\nfor (const event of events) {\n  if (event.prerequisites) {\n    for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n      adjList.get(prereqId)?.push(event.id);\n      inDegree.set(event.id, (inDegree.get(event.id) || 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but `inDegree.get(event.id)` is already set to 0 from initialization. So this should work:\n- Edge: prereqId -> eventId\n- adjList[prereqId] has eventId as neighbor\n- inDegree[eventId] increases\n\nBut wait, there's an issue: if a prerequisite ID doesn't exist in our events list, `adjList.get(prereqId)` returns undefined, so we don't add the edge. But we still increment the inDegree. This creates a node with an incoming edge from nowhere that can never be processed, so it stays stuck.\n\nActually, looking more carefully:\n\n```typescript\n// Initialize\nfor (const event of events) {\n  inDegree.set(event.id, 0);\n  adjList.set(event.id, []);\n}\n```\n\nSo only events in our list get initialized. If a prerequisite references an event that isn't in our events list, `adjList.get(prereqId)` is undefined, but we still increment inDegree[event.id]. This event will never reach inDegree 0 because the non-existent prerequisite is counted as an incoming edge.\n\nActually wait, if the prerequisite ID is not in our events list, we should probably still handle it. But the problem likely assumes all prerequisite IDs refer to valid events. Let me handle this by adding missing events as \"dummy\" events with no scheduling needs.\n\nActually, if a prerequisite references an event not in the list, it's technically an error or the event with the prereq can't be scheduled. Let me just throw an error or handle it gracefully. For now, let me assume all prerequisite IDs are valid event IDs.\n\nHmm, actually looking at my code again, the issue is more subtle. Let me re-examine:\n\n```javascript\nfor (const event of events) {\n  if (event.prerequisites) {\n    for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n      adjList.get(prereqId)?.push(event.id);\n      inDegree.set(event.id, (inDegree.get(event.id) || 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIf prereqId is not in the events list, `adjList.get(prereqId)` returns undefined, so we can't push the edge. But we still increment inDegree for event.id. This means event has a prereq that doesn't exist, and will never be processed.\n\nI think this is actually fine behavior - if an event has an invalid prerequisite that doesn't exist, it can't be scheduled. But actually, a simpler approach would be: if the prerequisite ID doesn't exist in our list, we just skip that prereq (treat it as satisfied since it doesn't constrain anything). Let me add a check:\n\nAct