nLet me implement a multi-phase greedy scheduler:\n\nPhase 1: Topological sort with constraint-based ordering\nPhase 2: Greedy placement (earliest start time in best-fit room)\nPhase 3: Backfill (try to place unscheduled events in remaining gaps)\n\nFor Phase 2, I'll sort events by these criteria (in order):\n1. Topological level (prerequisites first)\n2. Number of valid rooms (fewer = more constrained = earlier)\n3. Event duration (shorter = easier to place = schedule earlier to leave room for later... actually wait)\n   - Actually, placing large events first is often better because they're harder to fit in gaps\n   - But placing short events first maximizes flexibility for remaining space\n   - Hmm, I think placing short events first is generally better for maximizing count\n   - Actually, the classical scheduling wisdom says: shortest processing time first maximizes throughput\n4. Number of dependents (more dependents = earlier, to leave room for them)\n\nLet me go with: topological level, then duration (ascending), then capacity requirement (descending), then number of dependents (descending).\n\nWait, for maximizing the NUMBER of events (not total utility), shortest processing time first is indeed optimal for a single machine. For multiple machines/rooms with different capacities, it's more complex. Let me try: shortest duration first within the same topological level. This is a common scheduling heuristic.\n\nActually, for our problem, since rooms are heterogeneous (different capacities), we also need to consider room matching. Events with specific capacity needs should be carefully matched to rooms.\n\nLet me just implement it and iterate. Here's my plan:\n\n```\n1. Validate (cycle detection, duplicate IDs, non-existent prereqs)\n2. Topological sort\n3. For scheduling:\n   a. For each unscheduled event (in topo order):\n      - Find all valid placements\n      - Pick the best one (by heuristic: earlier start, better room fit)\n   b. At the end, try to backfill unscheduled events\n4. Return result\n```\n\nFor the \"best placement\" heuristic, I'll use:\n1. Prefer the preferred room if available\n2. Prefer earlier start times (leaves more room for later events)\n3. Prefer tight capacity fit (minimizes wasted capacity)\n\nLet me now write a clean, well-structured implementation.\n\nActually, I realize I need to think more carefully about the \"backfill\" step. After the initial greedy pass, some events may be unscheduled because there was no room at the time they needed. But later events might have freed up space. For example:\n\nSay we have events A, B, C all at the same topological level.\n- A (120 min, needs cap 10): scheduled R1 540-660\n- B (120 min, needs cap 10): scheduled R2 540-660\n- C (120 min, needs cap 10): no room available (only R1 and R2, both full)\n\nBut what if we had scheduled C instead of B?\n- A (120 min, needs cap 10): R1 540-660\n- C (120 min, needs cap 10): R2 540-660\n- B (120 min, needs cap 10): no room\n\nSame outcome. No problem here.\n\nBut what about:\n- A (120, needs cap 10)\n- B (60, needs cap 5)\n- C (120, needs cap 10)\n\nRooms: R1(cap 10), R2(cap 5)\nTime: 540-720 (180 min), gap 0\n\nOrdering: A, B, C (by duration ascending - B first):\n- B (cap 5): R2 540-600\n- A (cap 10): R1 540-660\n- C (cap 10): R1 660-720 (fits!)\nAll 3 scheduled.\n\nOrdering: A, C, B:\n- A (cap 10): R1 540-660\n- C (cap 10): R2 540-660 (wait, R2 has cap 5, C needs 10)\n  C can't go in R2. R1 is taken. C unscheduled.\n- B (cap 5): R2 540-600\nTotal: 2 scheduled, 1 unscheduled.\n\nSo ordering matters! Scheduling B first (the short event) is better because B can fit in many places and doesn't block C.\n\nIn my current approach, I sort by duration ascending within the same topological level, which handles this case correctly.\n\nLet me think of another case:\n- A (120, cap 10, prereq none)\n- B (120, cap 5, prereq none)\n- C (120, cap 10, prereq none)\n\nRooms: R1(cap 10), R2(cap 5)\nTime: 540-900, gap 0 (360 min)\n\n