e detected\n\nThis works correctly because the prerequisite relationship forms a directed dependency, and following prerequisites is following the \"depends on\" direction, which is the OPPOSITE of the edge direction in our adjacency list. But for cycle detection, following either direction works as long as we're consistent. Wait, no!\n\nLet me think of a simpler counterexample:\n- Edge: A → B (A is a prerequisite of B)\n- Edge: B → C (B is a prerequisite of C)\n- Edge: C → A (C is a prerequisite of A)\n\nCycle: A → B → C → A.\n\nMy adjacency list: A depends on nothing, B depends on A, C depends on B, A depends on C.\ninDegree: A=0, B=1, C=1. Wait, but A depends on C, so inDegree[A]=1.\n\nLet me redo:\n- Event B has prereq A: edge A → B\n- Event C has prereq B: edge B → C\n- Event A has prereq C: edge C → A\n\nAdjacency list: adj[A]=[B], adj[B]=[C], adj[C]=[A]\ninDegree: A=1, B=1, C=1\n\nNow, my DFS cycle detection follows prerequisites:\n- Start at B (prereq A): go B → A\n- A has prereq C: go A → C\n- C has prereq B: go C → B\n- B is already in stack → cycle!\n\nOK, it works because in this cycle, following the prerequisites (reverse of the edge direction) also forms a closed loop. In a directed cycle, following reverse edges also gives a directed cycle (just reversed). So both directions detect cycles correctly.\n\nAlright, the cycle detection is fine.\n\nNow let me think about one more edge case: what if an event's prerequisites include an event that's not in the events list?\n\nIf A depends on B, but B is not in the events list:\n- In topological sort: we should treat B as already satisfied (it doesn't need to be scheduled)\n- In cycle detection: B doesn't exist, so no cycle involving B\n\nLet me handle this in the topological sort by checking if the prereqId exists:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const event of events) {\n  if (event.prerequisites) {\n    for (const prereqId of event.prerequisites) {\n      if (!eventMap.has(prereqId)) continue; // missing prereq, trea